DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     _I_m_p_o_r_t_a_n_t: The functions eellff__ooppeenn() and eellff__ooppeennmmeemmoorryy() are extensions
     to the elf(3) API, for the internal use of the Elftoolchain project.
     Portable applications should not use these functions.

     The function eellff__ooppeenn() returns an Elf descriptor opened with mode
     ELF_C_READ for the ELF object or ar(1) archive referenced by the file de‐
     scriptor in argument _f_d.

     The function eellff__ooppeennmmeemmoorryy() returns an ELF descriptor opened with mode
     ELF_C_READ for the ELF object or ar(1) archive contained in the memory
     area pointed to by the argument _i_m_a_g_e.  The argument _s_z specifies the
     size of the memory area in bytes.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The function returns a pointer to a ELF descriptor if successful, or NULL
     if an error occurred.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     These functions are non-standard extensions to the elf(3) API set.

     The behavior of these functions differs from their counterparts
     elf_begin(3) and elf_memory(3) in that these functions will successfully
     open malformed ELF objects and ar(1) archives, returning an Elf descrip‐
     tor of type ELF_K_NONE.

EERRRROORRSS
     These functions can fail with the following errors:

     [ELF_E_ARGUMENT]  The argument _f_d was of an unsupported file type.

     [ELF_E_ARGUMENT]  The argument _s_z was zero, or the argument _i_m_a_g_e was
                       NULL.

     [ELF_E_IO]        The file descriptor in argument _f_d was invalid.

     [ELF_E_IO]        The file descriptor in argument _f_d could not be read.

     [ELF_E_RESOURCE]  An out of memory condition was encountered.

     [ELF_E_SEQUENCE]  Functions eellff__ooppeenn() or eellff__ooppeennmmeemmoorryy() was called be‐
                       fore a working version was established with
                       elf_version(3).

